# URL of the Leetify proxy Worker (worker/leetify-proxy.js). Leetify's public
# API sends no CORS headers, so a browser (including OBS's browser source) can't
# read its responses directly — the fetch fails with "Failed to fetch". This
# Worker adds CORS in front of it, so it's what makes Premier (Leetify) mode
# actually work in a browser. Like the other proxy URLs it's a public URL, not a
# secret; for the deployed site set a GitHub Actions repository variable named
# VITE_LEETIFY_PROXY_URL. Leave blank only for non-browser tooling — the widget
# then calls Leetify directly, which a browser will block.
VITE_LEETIFY_PROXY_URL=

# Leetify Public API key — optional, and only raises rate limits. When the
# Leetify proxy above is used the key belongs on the Worker instead (as its
# LEETIFY_KEY secret), so it stays out of the public bundle; this var is only
# read on the direct (no-proxy) fallback path. Get one from https://leetify.com
# (developer/API settings). Copy this file to `.env.local` for local dev.
VITE_LEETIFY_KEY=

# Optional: URL of the Steam avatar proxy Worker (see worker/README.md). When
# set, the widget shows the player's Steam avatar. This is just a public URL,
# not a secret, so it's safe to inline at build time. For the deployed site set
# a GitHub Actions repository variable named VITE_AVATAR_PROXY_URL. The Steam
# API key itself lives only on the Worker, never here.
VITE_AVATAR_PROXY_URL=

# Optional: URL of the FACEIT proxy Worker (see worker/README.md). Required for
# the customizer's FACEIT mode (the PREMIER | FACEIT toggle) — the widget calls
# it to resolve a FACEIT nickname to ELO, level, stats, and match history. Like
# the other proxy URLs this is a public URL, not a secret (the FACEIT API key
# lives only on the Worker); set a GitHub Actions repository variable named
# VITE_FACEIT_PROXY_URL for the deployed site. Leave blank to disable FACEIT mode.
VITE_FACEIT_PROXY_URL=

# Optional: PostHog analytics (see src/shared/analytics.ts). Create a free
# project at https://posthog.com, then paste its "Project API Key" (starts with
# `phc_`) here. When set, the customizer loads PostHog (cookie-based, but gated
# behind the consent banner) and the overlay loads it cookieless; leave blank to
# disable analytics entirely (the app works fine without it). The project key is
# a public, write-only identifier, not a secret. For the deployed site set a
# GitHub Actions repository variable named VITE_POSTHOG_KEY.
VITE_POSTHOG_KEY=
# PostHog API host — match your project's region: https://us.i.posthog.com (US,
# the default) or https://eu.i.posthog.com (EU). Set as a repository variable
# named VITE_POSTHOG_HOST for the deployed site.
#
# Reverse proxy (optional): to stop ad blockers dropping events, point this at a
# subdomain of your own domain instead, e.g. https://t.kapkit.ca — the app sends
# analytics there and it forwards to PostHog. Set up via PostHog's free Managed
# reverse proxy (a CNAME); see docs/ANALYTICS.md. Use a bare subdomain, no path.
VITE_POSTHOG_HOST=

# Optional: URLs of the live-status proxy Workers (one per platform — see
# worker/README.md). When a link the user pastes matches one of these platforms
# and its Worker URL is set, the win/loss becomes session-scoped (resets on
# go-live, freezes on offline). Each is a public URL, not a secret; the platform
# credentials live only on the Workers. For the deployed site set GitHub Actions
# repository variables with the same names. Any left blank simply disables that
# platform's live check (the widget falls back to rolling-window W/L).
VITE_TWITCH_PROXY_URL=
VITE_YOUTUBE_PROXY_URL=
VITE_KICK_PROXY_URL=
